Change per passage (what the representation did):
   1   2.5  ~~
          Socrates depicts chained cave-prisoners who can perceive only shadows and echoes,
          establishing them as like ourselves in enlightenment.
   2 12.75  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates shows that release from illusion begins with painful glare, confusion, and
          resistance, as the prisoner clings to familiar shadows before being dazzled by greater
          realities up to the sun.
   3 12.75  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates traces the freed prisoner's gradual ascent from shadows and reflections to
          the sun itself as cause of the visible world, leaving him to pity his former den and
          despise its shadow-honours.
   4 47.25  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates shows the returning philosopher blinded, ridiculed, and threatened with
          death, then explicitly decodes the cave as the visible world and crowns the ascent
          with the Idea of the Good.
   5  30.0  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates excuses the enlightened philosopher's unwillingness to return and ridiculous
          blindness in human affairs, then teaches that dazzlement comes from either entering
          light or darkness and should be diagnosed rather than mocked.
   6  20.5  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates rejects teachers who claim to insert knowledge into the soul, arguing that
          education is instead the art of turning the soul's innate power from becoming toward
          being and the Good.
   7  18.5  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates argues that keen natures freed from sensual drag could see truth sharply, so
          the founders must compel those who reach the Good to descend again and govern.
   8  20.0  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates answers Glaucon's charge of injustice by arguing that educated philosophers
          owe ruling service to the whole State, whose reluctant rulers will govern best.
   9  19.0  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates concludes that just philosophic pupils will accept ruling as a stern
          necessity, and only rulers given a better life of philosophy and rich in virtue can
          keep the State well-ordered.
Constructed meaning c03_1 (passage 4, inferred): Returning to darkness blinds the enlightened, making him seem incompetent at shadow-judging and turning the cave's defense of illusion from ridicule into willingness to kill the liberator.
    built from: Returnee blinded by darkness (provides the physiological basis: re-entry into
    darkness blinds the freed prisoner); Ridiculed shadow competitor (provides the social
    consequence: that blindness appears as ridiculous failure at shadow-judging); Death threat
    to liberator (provides the escalation from contempt to violence: prisoners would kill anyone
    attempting liberation)
Constructed meaning c03_2 (passage 4, inferred): The explicit decoding unifies chains, fire, ascent and sun as stages of the soul's passage from the visible world to the Idea of the Good as the ultimate author of beauty, right, light and truth.
    built from: Cave to intellect mapping (provides the explicit reframing code: prison is
    visible world, fire is sun, ascent is soul to intellect); Good as ultimate source (provides
    the culminating cause: Idea of the Good seen last as universal author of beauty, right,
    light and truth); Sun as World Guardian (provides the intermediate causality now
    subordinated: sun as guardian and cause of visibles); Seeing Sun Itself (provides the final
    vision now reinterpreted: seeing the sun itself as prefiguring seeing the Good)
Constructed meaning c04_1 (passage 5, inferred): Confused vision from either going into light or coming out of light must be diagnosed by direction rather than mocked, so the early retreat toward light is pitiable ascent while the philosopher's unwillingness and ridiculous blindness on return is happy descent.
    built from: Retreat from glaring light (provides the ascending instance of dazzle - eye-pain
    turning away toward familiar visibles); Philosopher unwilling to descend (provides the
    unwillingness to re-descend to be excused); Returner's courtroom blindness (provides the
    concrete ridiculous failure on return - blinking dispute about images of justice); Two kinds
    of blindness (provides the explanatory framework of two opposite causes of bewilderment for
    mind as for body); Suspend mockery and diagnose (provides the norm to suspend laughter and
    first diagnose direction of travel); Happy descent versus pity (provides the opposite
    verdicts - happy for descending dazzle, pity for ascending dazzle)
Constructed meaning c05_1 (passage 6, inferred): Cave bondage is not lack of sight but misdirection of an innate power, so liberation is education as the whole soul's turning from becoming to being rather than implantation of knowledge.
    built from: Chained Underground Prisoners (provides the bondage image reinterpreted as
    misdirected gaze rather than blindness); Painful forced liberation (provides the painful
    turning event reinterpreted as turning of the instrument of knowledge); Innate power of
    learning (provides the premise that capacity for learning already exists in the soul);
    Whole-soul turning toward being (provides the required direction of learning from becoming
    to being toward the Good); Art of easy conversion (provides the definition of education as
    reorienting existing sight rather than implanting sight)
Constructed meaning c06_1 (passage 7, inferred): Neither the ignorant nor perpetual contemplatives can govern, so those who would rather dwell in blessed isolation above must be compelled to descend and share the prisoners' labours and honours.
    built from: Neither Extreme Can Rule (states the disqualification of both extremes from
    ruling); Islands of the Blest (supplies the motive of endless learners fancying blessed
    isolation); Philosopher unwilling to descend (states the enlightened unwillingness to
    descend from longing to dwell above); Compelled Descent to Govern (supplies the
    institutional remedy of compelled return to shared labour and honours)
Constructed meaning c06_2 (passage 7, inferred): The same keen faculty that makes clever natures dangerously effective when dragged downward by sensual weights would see truth just as keenly if freed early and turned upward.
    built from: Leaden Weights of Pleasure (identifies appetitive pleasures as leaden drag
    pulling vision downward); Clever rogue's misdirected sight (exemplifies undiminished
    keenness serving evil when misdirected); Freed Faculty Sees Truth (states the counterfactual
    that the same faculty turned early would see truth as keenly)
Constructed meaning c07_1 (passage 8, inferred): Compelling philosophers who are unwilling to descend is not unjust because, bred to be hive rulers, their care serves the happiness of the whole State, and their very reluctance makes them the best and most quietly governed rulers.
    built from: Philosopher unwilling to descend (provides the unwillingness to descend that is
    revalued); Compelling Philosophers Is Just (provides the verdict that compulsion to care for
    others is not unjust); Happiness Of Whole State (provides the whole-state happiness aim that
    justifies the verdict); Philosophers As Hive Rulers (provides the bred-for-rule debt that
    grounds owed service); Reluctant Rulers Govern Best (revalues reluctance as the mark of the
    best quietly governed rule)
Constructed meaning c08_1 (passage 9, inferred): Because they are allowed a shared heavenly life of true philosophy, the initially unwilling philosophers will willingly take their turn at ruling as a stern necessity rather than refusing descent.
    built from: Philosopher unwilling to descend (provides initial unwillingness to descend from
    longing to dwell above); Compelled Descent to Govern (provides external demand to descend
    and share labours and honours); Pupils' Shared Heavenly Life (provides promised preponderant
    shared heavenly life alongside turns at toils); Ruling as Stern Necessity (provides
    resulting acceptance of office as stern necessity)
Constructed meaning c08_2 (passage 9, inferred): Only rulers who are rich in virtue and wisdom from a philosophical life that looks down on political ambition can be given a better life than ruling and so avoid the office-fighting for private gain that ruins the State.
    built from: Better Life For Rulers (states the requirement of a better life than ruling for
