Chained prisoners who mistake shadows for truth are painfully dragged up to the sun and the Good, then must learn to diagnose blindness, rethink learning as turning the soul, and accept reluctant return to rule for the good of all.

1. Chained Shadows and Painful Ascent  (passages 1-3)
    Prisoners who take shadows for reality are painfully freed and dragged upward to gradually
    see the sun.
       -> The completed climb to the sun sets up the shock of going back down and needing to
       explain what the story means.
2. Return Ridicule and the Good  (passages 4-4)
    The freed prisoner returns blind, is mocked and threatened with death, while the cave is
    decoded as the soul's journey to the Good.
       -> Ridicule of the blind returner and the decoding of the cave as the soul's journey
       demand a fairer way to judge confusion.
3. Diagnosing Blindness Rethinking Learning  (passages 5-6)
    Blindness from entering or leaving light must be diagnosed rather than mocked, and learning
    is turning the soul rather than inserting knowledge.
       -> Seeing learning as turning an existing power toward being raises the question of who
       will use that vision to govern.
4. Compelling the Enlightened to Descend  (passages 7-7)
    Since neither the ignorant nor those lost in contemplation can rule, the enlightened must be
    compelled to descend and share work.
       -> Ordering the enlightened to descend to share labour provokes the charge that
       compulsion is unjust.
5. Justice in Forcing Reluctant Rulers  (passages 8-8)
    Forcing philosophers to rule is just because they were raised for the whole, and the most
    reluctant govern best.
       -> Justifying reluctant rule for the happiness of the whole leads to how such rulers will
       actually live and accept office.
6. Accepting Rule as Stern Necessity  (passages 9-9)
    Philosophers sharing a heavenly life take turns ruling as a stern necessity, rich in virtue
    rather than ambition.
